course: dictionary
lesson: 495
title: Aside: one word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word aside can mean to one side, kept for later, or a short quiet remark. Context tells you which one. «Step aside, please, so the pushchair can pass.» Here aside means out of the way. Ask yourself what is happening in the sentence.
explain: «Saving means putting money aside, week after week, until you have enough.» Money put aside is kept for later, not spent now. Put aside, set aside and step aside all use aside, but the words next to it change the meaning.
explain: «Aside from the rain, the trip was perfect.» Aside from means except for, or as well as. Try your meaning in the sentence: "Except for the rain, the trip was perfect" still makes sense, so you have got it right.
explain: Aside can also be a noun, an aside, meaning a short comment that is off the main topic. In a play, an aside is words spoken to the audience that the other characters are not supposed to hear.
fact: Aside is an adverb when it tells you where something is or moves, as in step aside.
fact: Put aside and set aside mean keep for later, and they can be about money or time.
fact: Aside from means except for, or as well as.
fact: As a noun, an aside is a short remark that is off the main topic, or words spoken quietly to the audience in a play.
fact: A good check is to swap in a simple meaning, such as out of the way or kept for later, and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The frog was gently moved aside with wet hands.» What does aside mean here? | Kept for later | A quiet remark | Except for | *Out of the way
exercise: «Saving means putting money aside, week after week.» What does put aside mean? | Lose it | *Keep it for later | Spend it now | Throw it away
exercise: Mia says aside from means far away from. Which is correct? | Next to | Under | *Except for, or as well as | Far away from
test: «The elephant may step aside from anything that moves suddenly by its feet.» What does step aside mean? | Jump on a stool | Stand very still and loud | Run past a mouse | *Move out of the way
test: In a play, what is an aside? | A loud song at the end | A short sleep between scenes | A seat in the theatre | *Words spoken to the audience that other characters are not supposed to hear
exam: Which meaning fits aside in «Aside from the rain, the trip was perfect»? | A quiet remark | Very old | *Except for | To one side
